1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,440
Possibility at the height of the Cold War. How to stop it?

2
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:06,660
The heart of the answer lies inside this mountain.

3
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:10,380
It's a high-tech, bomb-proof, underground command center

4
00:00:10,381 --> 00:00:15,000
coordinating the largest array of military defense systems ever created.

5
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:21,140
Now, NORAD, the electronic sentinel, on Modern Marvels, then and now.

6
00:00:21,141 --> 00:00:27,640
High above Colorado Springs in the front range of the Rockies

7
00:00:27,641 --> 00:00:32,360
stands a 9,565-foot citadel of solid granite.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,040
It's Cheyenne Mountain.

9
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:41,200
Carved out of solid rock, a third of a mile below the surface

10
00:00:41,201 --> 00:00:43,680
in a man-made grid of chambers and tunnels

11
00:00:43,681 --> 00:00:46,580
designed to withstand a thermonuclear strike

12
00:00:46,581 --> 00:00:49,300
is the Cheyenne Mountain Operations Center.

13
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:54,860
Behind these hardened glass doors that weigh nearly 25 tons

14
00:00:54,861 --> 00:00:59,180
is a four and a half acre subterranean compound that is the largest and most

15
00:00:59,181 --> 00:01:01,320
complex command and control center

16
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:05,220
in the world. Over 1200 men and women

17
00:01:05,221 --> 00:01:10,020
including civilians work in Cheyenne Mountain. The Cheyenne Mountain complex

18
00:01:10,021 --> 00:01:13,160
was not only built for self-sufficiency and security

19
00:01:13,161 --> 00:01:16,300
it was literally built to rock and roll.

20
00:01:16,301 --> 00:01:20,480
The 15 buildings that make up the complex sit atop

21
00:01:20,481 --> 00:01:22,840
hundreds of three-foot-high steel springs.

22
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:29,700
These 1,000-pound steel coils were designed to absorb the seismic shockwaves of a thermonuclear

23
00:01:29,701 --> 00:01:31,640
strike or an earthquake.

24
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:39,840
Since 1966, the mountain has been the operation center for NORAD, the North American Aerospace

25
00:01:39,841 --> 00:01:45,329
Defense Command, and since 1985, America's newest military service, the U.S.

26
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:46,340
Space Command.

27
00:01:46,341 --> 00:01:52,340
NORAD continues to carry out a mission that began during the darkest days of the Cold War,

28
00:01:52,341 --> 00:01:58,340
when our national leaders feared the possibility of a surprise nuclear attack from the Soviet Union.

29
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:06,340
NORAD's mission is the air sovereignty, aerospace warning and aerospace control for North America.

30
00:02:06,341 --> 00:02:14,340
And since the Gulf War, Cheyenne Mountains' mission now includes the protection of American and Canadian forces around the world.

31
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:24,896
General Howell Estes III is the commander-in-chief of NORAD and the U.S.

32
00:02:24,897 --> 00:02:26,560
Space Command.

33
00:02:26,561 --> 00:02:32,140
He believes that despite the end of the Cold War, NORAD's mission is as vital as ever.

34
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:42,247
We have a responsibility as a nation, as does Canada, because NORAD is in fact a binational command between ourselves and the Canadians to provide for protection of

35
00:02:42,248 --> 00:02:44,180
the airspace over North America.

36
00:02:44,181 --> 00:02:49,520
That is a peacetime job. It would also be a job in crises.

37
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:52,720
In peacetime, we call this protecting our air sovereignty.

38
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:55,485
Though relations between the U.S.

39
00:02:55,486 --> 00:02:59,020
and the former Soviet Union have arguably never been better,

40
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:04,500
it is also a fact that Russia still possesses a tremendous nuclear arsenal

41
00:03:04,501 --> 00:03:06,920
and the means with which to deliver it.

42
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:13,940
And while at the moment we do not see a threat to our nation from the former Soviet Union,

43
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:17,940
The capability still resides in Russia today.

44
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:21,500
The intent clearly isn't there, but the capability is there.

45
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:26,760
And so to ensure that we can protect ourselves against the possibility of some change there,

46
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:30,560
which we do not contemplate, but nevertheless have to pay attention to,

47
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,780
we need to keep the system in existence.

48
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:37,400
One of the men most responsible for the day-to-day support of NORAD

49
00:03:37,401 --> 00:03:42,740
is Major General Jeff Grime, the commander of the Cheyenne Mountain Operations Center.

50
00:03:42,741 --> 00:03:49,440
A lot of people think there's a red button here and that we are the ones that push the button that would start the war.

51
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,080
Let me assure you, there are no red buttons that do those sorts of things here.

52
00:03:54,360 --> 00:04:00,420
We are in the warning business and we provide this information to our nation's leaders

53
00:04:00,421 --> 00:04:06,940
who then make the determination as to whether or not we wish to respond in kind.

54
00:04:06,941 --> 00:04:07,641
The U.S.

55
00:04:07,642 --> 00:04:15,540
Congress appropriated over $142 million to build the Cheyenne Mountain Complex back in 1961.

56
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:21,060
It would probably cost $18 to $20 billion to build it today.

57
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:28,800
However, some critics claim that maintaining this facility is too expensive and not necessary in a post-Cold War world.

58
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:32,960
They ask, is it not a relic of the Cold War?

59
00:04:32,961 --> 00:04:37,240
Or is it not extremely expensive to be operating in the mountain?

60
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:42,680
Actually, if you compare costs, the cost to operate in the mountain

61
00:04:42,681 --> 00:04:48,220
versus the cost to operate in a facility outside the mountain is very comparable.

62
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:54,140
NORAD's reach extends far beyond Cheyenne Mountain.

63
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:59,440
It is connected to a global network of warning and communication satellites,

64
00:04:59,441 --> 00:05:03,440
radar systems and deep space surveillance telescopes.

65
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:08,440
NORAD receives information from all around the world,

66
00:05:08,441 --> 00:05:13,440
from Thule, Greenland to tiny Altera in the middle of the Pacific Ocean.

67
00:05:13,441 --> 00:05:17,440
Brigadier General David W. Bartram, Canadian Forces,

68
00:05:17,441 --> 00:05:21,440
is the Vice Commander of the Cheyenne Mountain Operations Center.

69
00:05:21,441 --> 00:05:28,440
Cheyenne Mountain is a fusion center for data from air events, space events and missile events.

70
00:05:28,441 --> 00:05:32,960
Information comes from the satellites in space for ballistic missile warning.

71
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:37,160
Information from theater types of launches, like the SCUD,

72
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:41,840
is passed and detected by these same satellites and passed to Cheyenne Mountain.

73
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:47,980
Information on air events around North America comes into Cheyenne Mountain.

74
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:51,860
So in a nutshell, Cheyenne Mountain takes this information

75
00:05:51,861 --> 00:05:54,740
from this web of sensors around the world,

76
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:57,740
located in space and air on the ground and the sea,

77
00:05:57,741 --> 00:06:04,360
fuses that information and passes the information on to the National Command Authorities,

78
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,980
the President of the United States, the Prime Minister of Canada,

79
00:06:06,981 --> 00:06:10,920
and to our warfighting commanders stationed around the world.

80
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:17,240
Despite NORAD's dependence on sophisticated satellites and other high-tech equipment,

81
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:22,640
all data coming into the mountain is evaluated and verified by people.

82
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:29,020
Unlike the movie War Games, there are no supercomputers here that can circumvent their masters.

83
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:31,568
Lieutenant General L.W.F.

84
00:06:31,569 --> 00:06:35,940
Cuppins, Canadian Forces, is the deputy commander-in-chief of NORAD,

85
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:38,960
a position traditionally held by a Canadian.

86
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:44,480
What has made NORAD and our sister command, U.S. Space Command, so great,

87
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:49,260
as I've observed since I've come down here, is the people that work in it.

88
00:06:49,261 --> 00:06:55,240
You can give everyone wonderful computers, the best of surveillance systems, the best of information,

89
00:06:55,980 --> 00:07:01,460
but you need to have that human in the loop, making sure that your product is 100% right.

90
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:05,500
We cannot afford a NORAD to be wrong in our warning mission.

91
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:07,420
The consequences are too great.

92
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:13,940
Though it is doubtful that the Cheyenne Mountain Complex would survive a direct hit

93
00:07:13,941 --> 00:07:18,000
by one of today's very accurate high-yield nuclear warheads,

94
00:07:18,001 --> 00:07:25,000
It is still impervious to nearly every other weapon system and to any type of terrorist attack.

95
00:07:25,001 --> 00:07:33,000
The center has an air purification system designed to filter out nuclear, biological and chemical contaminants.

96
00:07:33,001 --> 00:07:43,000
Six big diesel generators to provide power and large stores of fuel, water and food that enable it to be thoroughly self-sufficient for up to 30 days.

97
00:07:43,001 --> 00:07:48,060
In a crisis, the center may be completely sealed off from the outside world.

98
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:57,060
The NORAD Command Center is supported by six other centers that are manned 24 hours a day.

99
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:00,740
The centers are the Air Defense Operations Center,

100
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:03,440
the Missile Warning Center,

101
00:08:04,260 --> 00:08:05,540
the Space Control Center,

102
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:08,680
the Combined Intelligence Watch Center,

103
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:10,880
the Systems Center,

104
00:08:10,881 --> 00:08:13,200
and the weather support unit.

105
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:19,880
Each has its own specific duties to help make it possible for NORAD to accomplish its mission.

106
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:24,280
A mission first begun when America found itself in a Cold War.

107
00:08:25,340 --> 00:08:29,720
In August of 1949, an event took place in the Soviet Union

108
00:08:29,721 --> 00:08:32,300
that threatened the very existence of North America.

109
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,440
The Russians had the bomb.

110
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:45,553
Eventually, the growing Soviet military threat brought the U.S.

111
00:08:45,554 --> 00:08:49,040
and Canada together in a defensive partnership.

112
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:56,280
In time, they would undertake what many believe is the greatest underground building project ever attempted.

113
00:08:57,780 --> 00:09:02,120
Before World War I, few countries had anything resembling an Air Force.

114
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:06,380
By 1990, 120 nations had established Air Forces.

115
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:10,940
NORAD will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels, Then and Now.

116
00:09:18,020 --> 00:09:22,458
22,300 miles above the Earth, the infrared sensor of a U.S.

117
00:09:22,459 --> 00:09:24,940
Defense Support Program satellite

118
00:09:24,941 --> 00:09:28,120
detects a heat source from a ballistic missile launch

119
00:09:28,121 --> 00:09:35,280
and transmits the data via high-speed processing sites to Durham, Saudi Arabia and Cheyenne Mountain, Colorado.

120
00:09:35,281 --> 00:09:39,280
This triggers a quick alert response at NORAD.

121
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:43,280
Missile launch from southern Iraq.

122
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:46,280
Sir, we have a missile event.

123
00:09:46,281 --> 00:09:47,280
Missile launch from southern Iraq.

124
00:09:47,281 --> 00:09:50,280
J.T. Joppa reporting on a missile event out of southern Iraq.

125
00:09:50,281 --> 00:09:52,280
Missile launch from southern Iraq.

126
00:09:52,281 --> 00:09:57,280
This is the CD-SYNC SED assessment on this event on the single SRBM is concerned.

127
00:09:57,281 --> 00:10:07,620
During Desert Storm, the Missile Warning Center in Cheyenne Mountain detected the launch of all 74 Scud missiles fired at coalition forces by Iraq.

128
00:10:09,960 --> 00:10:12,620
Predictive impact in Saudi Arabia.

129
00:10:12,700 --> 00:10:13,780
Sir, we have an impact.

130
00:10:14,020 --> 00:10:16,580
Predictive impact in Saudi Arabia.

131
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:21,320
King Khalid military city, Kuwait, Riyadh, and Saudi Arabia.

132
00:10:22,260 --> 00:10:24,960
Get along, get along, get along.

133
00:10:24,961 --> 00:10:34,494
Thanks to NORAD, within two and a half minutes of the launch, coalition troops are donning their protective gear and Patriot anti-missile batteries prepare to

134
00:10:34,495 --> 00:10:35,640
intercept the Scud.

135
00:10:35,641 --> 00:10:49,641
Though the Missile Warning Center was designed in the 1960s

136
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:55,180
to detect the launch of Soviet intercontinental ballistic missiles against North America,

137
00:10:55,360 --> 00:11:01,940
the warning system was modified to also detect the launch of small, short-range theater ballistic missiles

138
00:11:01,941 --> 00:11:04,100
like the Scud anywhere in the world.

139
00:11:04,101 --> 00:11:09,020
It's an example of how the mission inside Cheyenne Mountain has evolved over the years.

140
00:11:15,100 --> 00:11:20,140
NORAD was created in 1957 to deal with the growing Soviet military threat.

141
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:27,740
However, long before the communists came to power, the United States and Russia had fundamentally opposing philosophies.

142
00:11:27,741 --> 00:11:36,060
Russian civilization did not accept such basic Western concepts as democracy, individual rights or the free market.

143
00:11:39,140 --> 00:11:45,340
Relations between the two countries became even more strained after the 1917 Bolshevik Revolution.

144
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:50,280
But World War II would bring them together.

145
00:11:57,740 --> 00:12:03,140
By late 1941, fate would make the US and the USSR uneasy allies.

146
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:08,420
But this was strictly a marriage of convenience united by their mutual enmity of Hitler.

147
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:12,360
American and Soviet forces combined to defeat Nazi Germany.

148
00:12:12,361 --> 00:12:22,564
When the war ended, it became apparent that Russian dictator Joseph Stalin had no intention of giving up the territory now occupied by Soviet troops across Eastern and

149
00:12:22,565 --> 00:12:23,480
Central Europe.

150
00:12:23,481 --> 00:12:32,300
By 1946, the possibility of armed conflict between the former allies had escalated at flashpoints around the world.

151
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:34,000
U.S.

152
00:12:34,001 --> 00:12:42,020
and Soviet troops, or their surrogates, confronted each other in places like Turkey, Greece, Iran, Manchuria, and Germany.

153
00:12:42,180 --> 00:12:48,140
It was clear at that point that this uneasy alliance between the United States and the Soviet Union in the Second World War,

154
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:50,560
and in fact with all the allies, was falling apart.

155
00:12:50,561 --> 00:13:01,440
In June of 1948, the Soviets flexed their muscles and tested the resolve of their former allies by closing road and rail access to West Berlin through East Germany.

156
00:13:01,860 --> 00:13:08,480
The U.S., the U.K., and France initiated the Berlin Airlift to supply their troops and the people of West Berlin.

157
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:18,200
Eventually, the blockade was lifted, but the Soviets had demonstrated a willingness to back up their rhetoric for communist world domination with bold action.

158
00:13:18,201 --> 00:13:22,960
They were also making big technological advances in weapons systems.

159
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:28,280
Just after the Berlin airlift, as we progressed in the early 50s,

160
00:13:29,300 --> 00:13:35,960
the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics started developing a significant bomber capability

161
00:13:35,961 --> 00:13:40,900
with the ability to drop gravity bombs on any of their targets.

162
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:42,340
That was the technology of the day.

163
00:13:42,341 --> 00:13:52,921
And as that technology emerged, defense planners here in North America started looking at that threat and said, you know, the logical flight path of it would be from

164
00:13:52,922 --> 00:14:02,220
that country over the top, through the north, over Canada, into the strategic targets in the heartland of North America, both Canada and the U.S.

165
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:06,760
And they were worried because at that time there was not a defensive system for North America.

166
00:14:06,761 --> 00:14:12,080
And once again, as a result of our experience in the Second World War, focus on air defense seemed logical.

167
00:14:12,300 --> 00:14:17,900
The American people were going to demand some sort of defense for the North American continent.

168
00:14:21,740 --> 00:14:27,536
The primary responsibility for the air defense of the country was given to the newly formed U.S.

169
00:14:27,537 --> 00:14:28,237
Air Force.

170
00:14:28,900 --> 00:14:35,000
By the spring of 1949, they set up a makeshift early warning system in Alaska and the continental U.S.

171
00:14:35,001 --> 00:14:40,000
by linking 75 existing radar units together in a system nicknamed LASHA.

172
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:47,380
One of the significant factors in the continued development and cooperation between Americans and Canadians in terms of air defense

173
00:14:47,381 --> 00:14:50,020
was occasioned by the growing sense of threat.

174
00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:53,300
Soviet technology now had grown by leaps and bounds.

175
00:14:53,301 --> 00:14:59,980
If there was any doubt left that the USSR was a formidable adversary,

176
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:05,220
this was removed after it was revealed that the Soviets had detonated an atomic bomb

177
00:15:05,221 --> 00:15:08,740
years before anyone had thought they would have the capability.

178
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:13,260
The Russians were also developing new long-range jet bombers,

179
00:15:13,540 --> 00:15:17,240
which would have the capability of dropping A-bombs anywhere in the U.S.

180
00:15:17,860 --> 00:15:22,200
Defense planners in Washington now face the problem of defending the continent

181
00:15:22,201 --> 00:15:23,760
from a nuclear attack.

182
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:32,360
Obviously, the farther north enemy bombers could be detected and intercepted,

183
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:35,740
the more likely they could be prevented from reaching their targets.

184
00:15:36,260 --> 00:15:43,040
This made Canadian cooperation essential to the success of any early warning air defense plan for North America.

185
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:45,493
Fortunately, the U.S.

186
00:15:45,494 --> 00:15:49,680
and Canada had a tradition of working together on issues of mutual defense,

187
00:15:49,681 --> 00:15:51,480
going back to World War I.

188
00:15:51,940 --> 00:15:54,900
A joint U.S.-Canadian board studied the problem.

189
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:57,200
And the two nations, the thinkers of the time,

190
00:15:57,460 --> 00:15:59,000
both the political and military thinkers,

191
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:03,340
started looking at the possibility of how would we defend North America, our homeland.

192
00:16:03,620 --> 00:16:07,560
And the idea came about that we could design an air defense system

193
00:16:07,561 --> 00:16:11,920
that involved detection, surveillance, and if necessary, intercept

194
00:16:11,921 --> 00:16:15,300
and destruction of such targets cooperatively.

195
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:19,520
Basically the premise is two could do it less expensively than one.

196
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:27,720
On June 1, 1950, the first U.S.-Canada Emergency Air Defense Plan was approved by both nations.

197
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:33,620
As a first step, Canada would make its northernmost territory available for a radar system

198
00:16:33,621 --> 00:16:37,740
that would have been cost prohibitive for them to build and staff on their own.

199
00:16:38,480 --> 00:16:42,620
This system would be nicknamed the Dew Line for Distant Early Warning Line.

200
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:49,280
The location of the line would be between the Arctic Circle and Latitude 70 degrees north.

201
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:50,989
This would provide the U.S.

202
00:16:50,990 --> 00:16:54,140
and Canada with the earliest possible warning of a Soviet attack.

203
00:16:54,760 --> 00:16:57,620
The stations were numerous. The cost was very, very high.

204
00:16:58,300 --> 00:17:03,800
And, of course, the conditions under which these stations had to be placed were severe in the extreme.

205
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:07,820
Canada, especially up north, can be inhospitable in the winter.

206
00:17:07,821 --> 00:17:11,780
And we're talking about temperatures that certainly would freeze exposed human skin

207
00:17:11,781 --> 00:17:15,520
and test the vacuum tube technology of the 1950s to the limit.

208
00:17:15,521 --> 00:17:24,240
Despite tremendous hardships, the Dueline did get built, and by 1961 extended from the

209
00:17:24,241 --> 00:17:27,900
tip of Alaska's Aleutian Islands to Greenland.

210
00:17:27,901 --> 00:17:32,400
And basically what it was, was a tripwire system deployed in the high north of Canada

211
00:17:32,401 --> 00:17:36,880
along Alaska, along the coast of Labrador.

212
00:17:36,881 --> 00:17:41,180
And basically what it would do was, it didn't really track objects, it merely was like a

213
00:17:41,181 --> 00:17:42,180
beam.

214
00:17:42,181 --> 00:17:45,840
A bomber would come and penetrate that beam, and you knew something had passed.

215
00:17:46,460 --> 00:17:52,780
And then they relied on a series of radars further south in Canada called the Mid-Canada Line to guide interceptors.

216
00:17:55,300 --> 00:17:57,272
In 1957, members of the U.S.

217
00:17:57,273 --> 00:18:01,780
Continental Air Defense Command and the Royal Canadian Air Force

218
00:18:01,781 --> 00:18:03,766
jointly recommended that the U.S.

219
00:18:03,767 --> 00:18:08,640
and Canada could best counter the growing Soviet military threat to North America

220
00:18:08,641 --> 00:18:13,820
by forming an integrated defensive system with a combined staff from both nations.

221
00:18:14,700 --> 00:18:19,340
The new organization would be called the North American Air Defense Command, or NORAD.

222
00:18:19,900 --> 00:18:24,980
On September 12, 1957, military leaders from both the U.S. and Canada

223
00:18:24,981 --> 00:18:30,340
celebrated the establishment of NORAD at Ant Air Force Base in Colorado Springs.

224
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:33,480
However, their exultation was short-lived.

225
00:18:33,481 --> 00:18:43,980
Less than three weeks later, on October 4, 1957, the Soviets stunned the world by launching Sputnik,

226
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:47,380
the world's first man-made satellite, into Earth orbit.

227
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:53,300
In many places, the Soviets' great scientific achievements sparked talk of spaceships and moon travel.

228
00:18:53,301 --> 00:18:59,240
But to NORAD, it meant that it would just be a matter of time before the Soviet Union had the capability

229
00:18:59,241 --> 00:19:03,100
of lobbing nuclear weapons atop intercontinental missiles

230
00:19:03,101 --> 00:19:05,620
into a defenseless North America.

231
00:19:07,880 --> 00:19:12,360
The missile age required a whole new defensive strategy.

232
00:19:13,740 --> 00:19:15,400
But what should it be?

233
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:23,740
In early 1958, the U.S. and Canada were still reeling

234
00:19:23,741 --> 00:19:26,360
from the implications of the Sputnik launch.

235
00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:31,100
The balance of world power had shifted to the Soviet Union.

236
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:38,320
It would be just a matter of time before North America would be completely vulnerable to a ballistic missile attack.

237
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:47,080
Originally, the U.S.-Canadian air defense plan was to detect and intercept enemy bombers as far from their targets as possible.

238
00:19:47,560 --> 00:19:51,300
However, there was no way to intercept ICBMs.

239
00:19:51,740 --> 00:19:54,800
NORAD needed a new defensive strategy and fast.

240
00:19:54,801 --> 00:20:01,800
President Eisenhower had made it clear that our national defense policy would rest on a new doctrine,

241
00:20:01,801 --> 00:20:07,800
sometimes called the new look, which basically emphasized the role of the air forces and aerospace defense

242
00:20:07,801 --> 00:20:11,800
and long-range strategic bombardment to deter the Russians from attack.

243
00:20:11,801 --> 00:20:17,800
Eisenhower's strategists came to the conclusion that despite the expenditure of billions of dollars,

244
00:20:17,801 --> 00:20:23,420
billions of dollars, at a certain point the sheer number of nuclear weapons would overwhelm

245
00:20:23,421 --> 00:20:27,773
any sort of defensive system, including an anti-ballistic system.

246
00:20:27,774 --> 00:20:29,380
Therefore, they reasoned

247
00:20:29,381 --> 00:20:33,620
the best defense was a powerful offense.

248
00:20:33,621 --> 00:20:39,920
For the new look strategy to work, it was essential that the President, the Prime Minister

249
00:20:39,921 --> 00:20:45,260
of Canada and military command authorities be given the earliest possible warning that

250
00:20:45,261 --> 00:20:47,113
North America was under attack.

251
00:20:47,114 --> 00:20:50,940
If not, America's offensive strike force could be wiped out in a

252
00:20:50,941 --> 00:20:53,305
preemptive nuclear first strike.

253
00:20:53,306 --> 00:20:57,740
The idea was to deter a Soviet attack by preserving the U.S.

254
00:20:57,840 --> 00:21:00,669
capability for massive retaliation.

255
00:21:00,670 --> 00:21:05,680
It was around this time that NORAD's fundamental mission began

256
00:21:05,681 --> 00:21:11,275
to change from air defense to detection, assessment, and warning.

257
00:21:11,276 --> 00:21:14,460
Now, NORAD needed a radar system that

258
00:21:14,461 --> 00:21:22,620
could detect and track ballistic missiles to their targets.

259
00:21:22,621 --> 00:21:28,580
In early 1958, NORAD headquarters were located in a residential neighborhood in Colorado

260
00:21:28,581 --> 00:21:33,960
Springs in an old brick building on the grounds of and Air Force Base.

261
00:21:33,961 --> 00:21:38,640
In addition to being much too small to manage the growing air defense system, the building

262
00:21:38,641 --> 00:21:44,420
was highly vulnerable to conventional attack or even sabotage, let alone a nuclear strike.

263
00:21:47,640 --> 00:21:52,000
NORAD's underground combat operations center was a result of a number of factors,

264
00:21:52,200 --> 00:21:54,400
not the least of which was the perceived threat.

265
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:59,920
When the Soviets exploded their hydrogen bomb and demonstrated a capability to marry that

266
00:21:59,921 --> 00:22:05,820
to long-range ballistic missiles, the secure command and control facility seemed essential.

267
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:11,880
The Air Force was given the somewhat incredible task of creating a hardened site

268
00:22:11,881 --> 00:22:16,060
or an underground base from which the NORAD Combat Operations Center

269
00:22:16,061 --> 00:22:19,900
could survive and operate in after a nuclear attack.

270
00:22:21,060 --> 00:22:23,500
After a thorough study of potential sites,

271
00:22:23,640 --> 00:22:26,520
the Army Corps of Engineers recommended Cheyenne Mountain

272
00:22:26,521 --> 00:22:28,600
in the front range of the Rocky Mountains.

273
00:22:30,300 --> 00:22:33,560
Just four miles southwest of Colorado Springs,

274
00:22:33,561 --> 00:22:39,360
Cheyenne Mountain was near existing communication lines and supporting military installations.

275
00:22:40,200 --> 00:22:44,920
Most importantly, a geological examination indicated that the mountain's granite rock

276
00:22:44,921 --> 00:22:48,660
would offer the greatest degree of protection against a thermonuclear blast

277
00:22:48,661 --> 00:22:52,320
in the shortest possible construction time and at the lowest cost.

278
00:22:53,140 --> 00:22:56,280
The excavation and creation of the Cheyenne Mountain complex

279
00:22:56,281 --> 00:23:00,500
would arguably be the greatest underground building project ever attempted.

280
00:23:00,501 --> 00:23:12,500
The Air Force design required a 253,000 square foot facility of free standing buildings beneath 2,000 feet of solid granite.

281
00:23:13,500 --> 00:23:24,500
After an access road was built, the first phase of excavation began on June 20th, 1961, initiated by a ceremonial first blast.

282
00:23:24,501 --> 00:23:25,201
blast.

283
00:23:25,202 --> 00:23:31,500
To give some idea of how desperate NORAD was to complete the new hardened center,

284
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:38,000
they insisted that work be done in three shifts, 24 hours a day, six days a week, until the

285
00:23:38,001 --> 00:23:39,989
excavation was completed.

286
00:23:39,990 --> 00:23:45,000
Over 1,800,000 pounds of high explosives were used to blast the

287
00:23:45,001 --> 00:23:49,773
rock away. More than 693,000 tons of granite were excavated.

288
00:23:49,774 --> 00:23:52,000
It took only 300,000 tons of

289
00:23:54,500 --> 00:24:00,380
had 67 days to do the job. The excavation itself, the building of the tunnels within

290
00:24:00,381 --> 00:24:03,569
the mountain was an extremely large project.

291
00:24:03,570 --> 00:24:06,540
It was run by the Army Corps of Engineers

292
00:24:06,541 --> 00:24:13,120
and they did an exceptionally magnificent job in accomplishing that. Great care was

293
00:24:13,121 --> 00:24:18,700
taken in order not to blast away more rock than the excavation design called for. An

294
00:24:18,701 --> 00:24:23,680
innovative blasting technique called smooth wall blasting was used during excavation to

295
00:24:23,681 --> 00:24:27,680
to prevent excessive fracturing or weakening of the granite.

296
00:24:27,681 --> 00:24:32,380
To ensure that rock throughout the excavation would remain structurally sound,

297
00:24:32,381 --> 00:24:37,980
the walls of every cavern were reinforced with a system of rock bolts.

298
00:24:37,981 --> 00:24:42,680
Placed in a four-foot pattern, these six-foot to thirty-two-foot long steel bolts

299
00:24:42,681 --> 00:24:50,680
hold the rock together and provide the tension to withstand the shock effects of a nuclear detonation.

300
00:24:50,681 --> 00:24:53,960
Chain-link mesh was then fastened to these rock bolts.

301
00:24:54,540 --> 00:24:58,860
The wire mesh prevents sprawling or the natural loosening of small rock fragments.

302
00:24:59,460 --> 00:25:03,600
More importantly, it would catch any rock shaken loose by a nuclear blast.

303
00:25:04,560 --> 00:25:06,940
There are two entrances into the mountain.

304
00:25:07,660 --> 00:25:14,060
The north portal leads to a 1,500-foot-long tunnel, and the south portal runs for 2,500 feet.

305
00:25:14,061 --> 00:25:22,060
Both tunnels open into the central access tunnel which is a thousand feet long, 25 feet high and 40 feet wide.

306
00:25:22,061 --> 00:25:26,060
The north and south tunnel entrances serve as air intakes,

307
00:25:26,061 --> 00:25:34,060
but more significantly they were designed to allow a nuclear blast a way to pass through one end of the tunnel and out the other.

308
00:25:36,060 --> 00:25:40,060
The main complex is composed of seven major chambers in a grid pattern.

309
00:25:40,061 --> 00:25:46,728
grid pattern. These chambers are 60 and a half feet high and 56 feet wide.

310
00:25:46,729 --> 00:25:48,260
Phase two, or the

311
00:25:48,261 --> 00:25:52,512
build-back phase of the project, began in early 1964.

312
00:25:52,513 --> 00:25:55,400
Eleven buildings were constructed of

313
00:25:55,401 --> 00:26:02,606
continuously rolled 3-8 inch steel. Most of the buildings were three stories high.

314
00:26:02,607 --> 00:26:03,660
Each one was

315
00:26:03,661 --> 00:26:08,500
welded into a self-contained box to protect sensitive electronic equipment from the effects

316
00:26:08,501 --> 00:26:15,500
of an EMP or electromagnetic pulse that is generated during a nuclear blast.

317
00:26:19,460 --> 00:26:24,620
An airlock system was put in place to filter out the effects of nuclear, biological or

318
00:26:24,621 --> 00:26:26,298
chemical contamination.

319
00:26:26,299 --> 00:26:31,260
Blast valves and doors were designed to prevent the effects of blast

320
00:26:31,261 --> 00:26:38,260
pressure and were designed to close automatically by sensing changes in the outside air pressure.

321
00:26:38,500 --> 00:26:48,965
All utility conduits were joined at key junctions in the flexible type of configuration which allows pipes and lines to easily sway without breaking in the event of a

322
00:26:48,966 --> 00:26:50,100
seismic shockwave.

323
00:26:50,500 --> 00:26:56,500
Every system in the mountain was also constructed with a triple redundancy, including power.

324
00:26:56,501 --> 00:27:06,100
With construction completed in early 1965, NORAD began the process of installing their

325
00:27:06,101 --> 00:27:12,100
computers, displays and communications equipment into the new Subterranean Combat Control Center.

326
00:27:13,480 --> 00:27:17,240
Linking everything together and making it all work was not going to be easy.

327
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:21,420
But military planners believed that our lives depended on it.

328
00:27:21,421 --> 00:27:27,140
The Soviet Union had already initiated the largest nuclear weapons buildup in history.

329
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:34,640
Two non-commissioned officers at NORAD tighten bolts three times a day

330
00:27:34,641 --> 00:27:38,000
to provide strength to the rock walls of Cheyenne Mountain.

331
00:27:38,720 --> 00:27:42,420
NORAD will return on Modern Marvels, then and now.

332
00:27:42,421 --> 00:27:55,100
In January of 1966, the new NORAD Combat Operations Center achieved its initial operating capability.

333
00:27:55,940 --> 00:28:01,240
Cheyenne Mountain had now become the all-knowing, all-seeing sentinel for North America.

334
00:28:01,241 --> 00:28:02,360
We have an unknown.

335
00:28:03,960 --> 00:28:04,660
Quick alert!

336
00:28:04,661 --> 00:28:05,361
Quick alert!

337
00:28:05,380 --> 00:28:06,080
Quick alert!

338
00:28:06,580 --> 00:28:07,280
What do you do?

339
00:28:07,281 --> 00:28:08,040
This one is shooting.

340
00:28:08,360 --> 00:28:09,060
Thank you.

341
00:28:09,061 --> 00:28:09,761
Please.

342
00:28:09,762 --> 00:28:13,180
Missile warning is warning. Quick alert. The site appears to be...

343
00:28:13,181 --> 00:28:16,180
Roger, understand. I'll launch from the Sardis Union. Roger.

344
00:28:16,181 --> 00:28:20,180
Sir, I think NORAD assessment is required. Need a system report?

345
00:28:20,181 --> 00:28:23,782
System report is valid. Missile warning recommends a no assessment.

346
00:28:23,783 --> 00:28:25,180
This is a routine warning.

347
00:28:25,181 --> 00:28:27,180
Roger, understand. Sorry to report about...

348
00:28:27,181 --> 00:28:35,180
Over 30 years later, the new NORAD Command Center continues to evaluate quick alerts every time a missile is launched somewhere in the world.

349
00:28:35,181 --> 00:28:42,180
The command center is fed information from six other centers in and around Cheyenne Mountain.

350
00:28:42,181 --> 00:28:49,180
The system center confirms that all communications and computer systems are operating correctly

351
00:28:49,181 --> 00:28:55,180
so that what the people in the command center and other centers are seeing on their screens is accurate.

352
00:28:55,181 --> 00:29:01,180
NORAD's computers are all part of a closed-loop system that cannot be accessed by hackers.

353
00:29:01,181 --> 00:29:08,378
The Combined Intelligence Watch Center gathers intelligence information from other U.S.

354
00:29:08,379 --> 00:29:09,079
sources,

355
00:29:09,500 --> 00:29:14,820
including surveillance satellites, to assist the command center in correlating and analyzing

356
00:29:14,821 --> 00:29:16,315
events around the world.

357
00:29:16,316 --> 00:29:20,300
For example, they often warn the command center before countries

358
00:29:20,301 --> 00:29:23,192
about to test a ballistic missile.

359
00:29:23,193 --> 00:29:28,040
The Weather Support Unit performs two valuable functions.

360
00:29:28,041 --> 00:29:32,440
They monitor geophysical and space weather elements such as solar flares

361
00:29:32,441 --> 00:29:36,720
that may affect the performance of the Defense Support Program warning satellites.

362
00:29:38,760 --> 00:29:42,060
Now our satellites in space will pick up that solar flare,

363
00:29:42,360 --> 00:29:47,600
and it could look like the launch of an ICBM or a submarine-launched ballistic missile.

364
00:29:48,500 --> 00:29:53,080
But our people are smart enough to know that before they would say that's an SLBM,

365
00:29:53,340 --> 00:29:58,020
they'll talk with intelligence to see if they have an understanding of where submarines may be.

366
00:29:58,040 --> 00:30:01,360
they'll talk with the weather people, who in this case would say,

367
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:05,740
you have a solar flare that's glinting off of a calm ocean surface,

368
00:30:05,940 --> 00:30:08,980
and it may look like a missile launch.

369
00:30:09,600 --> 00:30:14,480
The Weather Support Unit also continuously monitors the weather in North America.

370
00:30:15,080 --> 00:30:19,040
In some instances, the unit will confirm that severe weather could be the reason

371
00:30:19,041 --> 00:30:23,240
that a plane is not on a planned flight path and appears as an unknown.

372
00:30:24,500 --> 00:30:27,560
In 1981, NORAD's name changed.

373
00:30:28,040 --> 00:30:34,120
Though its acronym stayed the same, NORAD's name was altered from North American Air Defense Command

374
00:30:34,121 --> 00:30:42,620
to North American Aerospace Defense Command to reflect its increased responsibility in the area of space surveillance and control.

375
00:30:43,800 --> 00:30:48,880
Since 1985, the U.S. Space Command has supported NORAD in these two missions.

376
00:30:48,881 --> 00:30:52,760
The U.S. Space Command provides the DSP constellation.

377
00:30:52,761 --> 00:30:59,920
They provide the early warning radars, the ballistic warning radars, and the submarine-launched ballistic missile radars,

378
00:31:00,500 --> 00:31:08,840
plus a very elaborate intelligence system that is able to tap sources worldwide through other means that may be space-based

379
00:31:08,841 --> 00:31:11,640
to assist in confirming what these objects are.

380
00:31:12,480 --> 00:31:13,283
So without the U.S.

381
00:31:13,284 --> 00:31:16,580
Space Command, it would be impossible for NORAD to do that particular mission.

382
00:31:16,581 --> 00:31:22,923
U.S. Space Command is a unified command composed of members of all of the U.S.

383
00:31:22,924 --> 00:31:23,980
armed forces.

384
00:31:24,580 --> 00:31:27,980
They also operate the Space Control Center in Cheyenne Mountain.

385
00:31:28,140 --> 00:31:30,920
The primary mission of the Space Control Center is space surveillance.

386
00:31:31,400 --> 00:31:35,160
We're responsible for tracking all man-made objects that rotate around planet Earth.

387
00:31:35,500 --> 00:31:38,740
We've been doing this since 1957 when Russia put up Sputnik.

388
00:31:38,900 --> 00:31:40,600
That was catalog object number one.

389
00:31:40,601 --> 00:31:49,860
The newest object that we're currently tracking on, some 40 years later, is object number 24,808, which is a Norwegian communications satellite.

390
00:31:50,760 --> 00:31:57,880
Objects are tracked with both radar sensors and deep space electro-optical sensors that work much like a telescope.

391
00:31:59,140 --> 00:32:05,760
Incredibly, the near-Earth sensors can track objects the size of this bolt, 600 miles above the Earth.

392
00:32:05,761 --> 00:32:12,360
On this display is the 24 satellite GPS constellation, which is a navigation constellation owned by the United States.

393
00:32:12,900 --> 00:32:19,460
Each of these satellites is indicated by a number, and the space trace is indicated by this line.

394
00:32:20,180 --> 00:32:24,160
You can imagine what this would look like with 8,000 objects that we're currently tracking on.

395
00:32:25,360 --> 00:32:32,060
The Space Control Center has supported every space shuttle mission by providing collision avoidance information to NASA.

396
00:32:32,061 --> 00:32:38,600
Ironically, the center now provides the same type of information to our former adversaries

397
00:32:38,601 --> 00:32:42,140
in Russia in support of the Mir space station.

398
00:32:42,141 --> 00:32:47,180
The Space Control Center is also responsible for re-entry assessments or the prediction

399
00:32:47,181 --> 00:32:52,440
of when and where an object will re-enter the atmosphere.

400
00:32:52,441 --> 00:32:54,240
This is done for two reasons.

401
00:32:54,241 --> 00:32:59,180
First of all, the Space Control Center needs to have an up-to-date catalog of what is in

402
00:32:59,181 --> 00:33:01,060
space.

403
00:33:01,061 --> 00:33:05,900
The second reason is to comply with the 1971 nuclear risk reduction treaty between the

404
00:33:05,901 --> 00:33:09,020
US and the former Soviet Union.

405
00:33:09,021 --> 00:33:13,360
If it appears that an object will re-enter over Russia, they are notified in advance

406
00:33:13,361 --> 00:33:21,800
so that they do not mistake it for an ICBM's warhead.

407
00:33:21,801 --> 00:33:26,560
The Missile Warning Center inside the Cheyenne Mountain Complex has an equally important

408
00:33:26,561 --> 00:33:27,920
mission.

409
00:33:27,921 --> 00:33:32,520
Their job is to report on space launches, strategic and missile launches, and nuclear

410
00:33:32,521 --> 00:33:36,680
detonations around the world.

411
00:33:36,681 --> 00:33:43,280
One of their most important tools is the DSP, or Defense Support Program satellite.

412
00:33:43,281 --> 00:33:48,520
These two and a half ton satellites use infrared sensors to detect the heat signature of a

413
00:33:48,521 --> 00:33:51,680
ballistic missile during its boost phase.

414
00:33:51,681 --> 00:33:58,540
They are on station approximately 22,300 miles above the equator in a geostationary orbit

415
00:33:58,541 --> 00:34:01,640
and are spaced to provide overlapping coverage.

416
00:34:01,641 --> 00:34:05,280
Because they are traveling at the same speed as the rotating Earth,

417
00:34:05,281 --> 00:34:12,220
they essentially remain in the same location at all times like ever-present electronic sentinels.

418
00:34:12,221 --> 00:34:18,980
In addition to the DSPs, the Missile Warning Center also utilizes the Ballistic Missile Early Warning System,

419
00:34:18,981 --> 00:34:22,020
which is built in the early 1960s.

420
00:34:22,021 --> 00:34:27,920
These three sites at Clear Alaska, Tule Greenland and Filingdales, England provide overlapping

421
00:34:27,921 --> 00:34:35,040
coverage zones that extend over 2,600 miles into the Soviet Union's Arctic regions.

422
00:34:35,041 --> 00:34:37,790
Two more sites on the east and west coasts of the U.S.

423
00:34:37,791 --> 00:34:39,420
provide coverage of the Atlantic

424
00:34:39,421 --> 00:34:42,020
and Pacific Oceans respectively.

425
00:34:42,021 --> 00:34:47,600
They are on the lookout for sea-launched ballistic missiles or SLBMs.

426
00:34:47,601 --> 00:34:53,780
This second independent system makes it possible to verify a missile event by a dual phenomenology

427
00:34:53,781 --> 00:34:54,780
method.

428
00:34:54,781 --> 00:34:59,680
And by dual phenomenology, what we're talking about is using two different types of technologies,

429
00:34:59,681 --> 00:35:06,880
two different types of systems to confirm that in fact a missile event has occurred.

430
00:35:06,881 --> 00:35:10,700
For example, when the missile is first launched from wherever that may occur, it's going to

431
00:35:10,701 --> 00:35:15,880
be picked up by our defense support program satellites.

432
00:35:15,881 --> 00:35:21,920
But before we would say that North America is under attack, we require a confirmation

433
00:35:21,921 --> 00:35:27,100
of that from our surveillance radars that we have around North America, hence the term

434
00:35:27,101 --> 00:35:29,240
dual phenomenology.

435
00:35:29,241 --> 00:35:34,880
The Missile Warning Center is responsible for evaluating incoming missile event information

436
00:35:34,881 --> 00:35:37,200
and quickly passing it down the line.

437
00:35:37,201 --> 00:35:41,640
So from the time a satellite picks up a launch worldwide, somebody processes that on the

438
00:35:41,641 --> 00:35:45,840
ground, sends it to us here in the Missile Warning Center, we inform the command center,

439
00:35:45,841 --> 00:35:49,460
The command center makes an assessment, and we inform national command authorities,

440
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:51,420
has to happen in under four minutes.

441
00:35:52,120 --> 00:35:56,600
On average, the missile warning center monitors over 200 missile events a year.

442
00:35:57,440 --> 00:36:01,020
The center is divided into theater and strategic missile sections.

443
00:36:01,620 --> 00:36:06,440
The strategic missile warning system evaluates any launch that may be a threat to North America,

444
00:36:06,980 --> 00:36:13,060
while the theater section monitors areas where U.S., Canadian, or Allied forces may be at risk.

445
00:36:13,061 --> 00:36:20,060
If a missile can travel more than 2100 nautical miles, it's considered a strategic weapon.

446
00:36:20,061 --> 00:36:24,060
The biggest difference between a strategic and a theater missile launch is the flight time.

447
00:36:24,061 --> 00:36:29,060
While an ICBM might require 30 minutes or more to go from one continent to another,

448
00:36:29,061 --> 00:36:34,060
a theater missile like the Scud might only be in the air for a few minutes.

449
00:36:34,061 --> 00:36:41,060
Therefore theater commanders receive notification of a missile launch directly from DSP satellites.

450
00:36:41,061 --> 00:36:48,060
NORAD then provides additional details such as the time and location of an impact.

451
00:36:48,061 --> 00:36:55,060
The third major warning center that contributes to the NORAD mission is the Air Defense Operations Center.

452
00:36:55,061 --> 00:37:04,060
Once NORAD's principal component, their responsibility today is the command and control for the Air Surveillance and Air Defense Network for North America.

453
00:37:04,061 --> 00:37:11,000
In 1989, the Air Defense Operations Center was asked to help win a new kind of war,

454
00:37:11,720 --> 00:37:15,980
one being fought in nearly every town and city in North America.

455
00:37:25,440 --> 00:37:32,240
Over 7,000 planes transit into North America every day, and every one must be accounted for.

456
00:37:32,241 --> 00:37:38,720
Most planes are identified by flight plan information provided by the FAA or Transport Canada.

457
00:37:39,720 --> 00:37:46,980
In an average year, there are approximately 800 cases that require NORAD's assistance before a positive ID can be made.

458
00:37:48,080 --> 00:37:53,520
In 10 to 15 percent of these events, the unknown aircraft are suspected narcotic smugglers.

459
00:37:54,900 --> 00:37:59,760
Since 1989, NORAD has joined the war against illegal drugs.

460
00:38:00,340 --> 00:38:03,080
NORAD takes no action against these planes itself.

461
00:38:03,540 --> 00:38:08,300
Their responsibility is to contact the appropriate law enforcement agencies and offer support.

462
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:17,940
An incident in September of 1996 typifies the type of binational and interagency cooperation necessary to stop determined smugglers.

463
00:38:19,220 --> 00:38:25,760
Initially, an aircraft was detected here in the Caribbean, and that aircraft could not be initially identified.

464
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:30,980
and a contact was made with the customs folks to find out if they were interested in that aircraft

465
00:38:30,981 --> 00:38:33,620
as possibly being involved in drug smuggling.

466
00:38:34,240 --> 00:38:40,080
Customs expressed an interest in that, and we began to cooperate with them in sharing information.

467
00:38:40,740 --> 00:38:44,000
NORAD's radar assets, in this case a Navy ship,

468
00:38:44,240 --> 00:38:50,400
allowed them to pick up the unknown aircraft long before it would be in range of NORAD's land-based radar system.

469
00:38:51,380 --> 00:38:54,680
The U.S. Coast Guard then provided an airborne radar platform

470
00:38:54,681 --> 00:38:58,780
to track the suspicious plane until a customs aircraft could take over.

471
00:39:00,180 --> 00:39:03,660
U.S. Customs Domestic Air Interdiction Coordinating Center

472
00:39:03,661 --> 00:39:08,720
asked NORAD's Canadian region to scramble two CF-18 interceptors

473
00:39:08,721 --> 00:39:12,420
to keep track of the suspicious craft as it approached Canada.

474
00:39:13,720 --> 00:39:17,600
U.S. Customs also notified the Royal Canadian Mounted Police,

475
00:39:17,980 --> 00:39:19,920
who joined the pursuit by helicopter.

476
00:39:20,400 --> 00:39:26,460
By the time the plane landed in Quebec, the bales of cocaine tossed from the plane had been retrieved,

477
00:39:26,640 --> 00:39:28,720
and the smugglers were about to be arrested.

478
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:33,020
It's just a part of our mission here in NORAD.

479
00:39:33,100 --> 00:39:39,480
We want to identify, track, and if necessary, intercept every unknown aircraft that comes into NORAD.

480
00:39:39,900 --> 00:39:43,540
If they're bringing drugs in, then it's just a natural thing for us to detect them

481
00:39:43,541 --> 00:39:46,960
and pass them off to the law enforcement people to take the appropriate action.

482
00:39:50,400 --> 00:40:02,700
Air Defense Command's job is more challenging today than ever.

483
00:40:03,440 --> 00:40:07,100
One of the reasons is due to the global proliferation of cruise missiles.

484
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:13,120
They're fast, stealthy, low-flying missiles that could be armed with a nuclear warhead.

485
00:40:13,121 --> 00:40:18,080
They are very accurate and could be launched from a great distance by enemy ships or bombers.

486
00:40:18,081 --> 00:40:21,960
They are extremely difficult to detect because of their size.

487
00:40:22,620 --> 00:40:25,000
Very, very small radar cross-section.

488
00:40:26,300 --> 00:40:27,620
So you have two challenges.

489
00:40:27,800 --> 00:40:30,520
One is to track the launching platform,

490
00:40:31,040 --> 00:40:33,480
and the second part is to track the missile itself.

491
00:40:33,820 --> 00:40:37,600
It's a technological challenge, and NORAD has been adapting to meet that challenge.

492
00:40:37,860 --> 00:40:38,560
U.S.

493
00:40:38,561 --> 00:40:42,720
and Canadian leaders are quite concerned about the proliferation of cruise and ballistic missiles.

494
00:40:42,721 --> 00:40:48,640
NORAD's mission has become more daunting as production and sales of cruise missiles is expanding in foreign countries,

495
00:40:48,920 --> 00:40:52,080
and more than 30 nations already possess ballistic missiles.

496
00:40:52,480 --> 00:40:56,000
And while today we don't anticipate a threat against our shores,

497
00:40:56,200 --> 00:40:59,920
in the future, as these missiles become more and more prevalent,

498
00:41:00,040 --> 00:41:05,960
not only to nations, but to terrorist groups, the risk to our country increases.

499
00:41:06,500 --> 00:41:08,060
We just need to think about this.

500
00:41:08,180 --> 00:41:10,220
I'm not saying we need to build a defense tomorrow.

501
00:41:10,221 --> 00:41:19,180
I'm just saying that's a job we have in NORAD to protect the borders of our country and the airspace above our nation and above Canada.

502
00:41:19,820 --> 00:41:24,320
And that's what NORAD's future is, worrying about ballistic missiles from rogue nations

503
00:41:24,321 --> 00:41:29,300
and worrying about the tremendous expansion and the proliferation of cruise missiles

504
00:41:29,301 --> 00:41:32,080
and the risk to our country from those cruise missiles.

505
00:41:32,760 --> 00:41:38,180
The time to start worrying about it and thinking about it is now, not after the first one lands on our soil.

506
00:41:40,220 --> 00:41:47,040
Though the concept of mutually assured destruction helped deter war with the former Soviet Union,

507
00:41:47,420 --> 00:41:51,640
that kind of deterrence strategy would have little or no effect on terrorists.

508
00:41:52,240 --> 00:41:56,860
And there's always the worry of a missile being accidentally launched from somewhere.

509
00:41:58,060 --> 00:42:03,180
Interestingly enough, I find that in discussions with people around the country,

510
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:08,660
and we take surveys, we find that about 70% of our population in our country

511
00:42:08,661 --> 00:42:10,720
thinks that we have a missile defense system today.

512
00:42:11,460 --> 00:42:15,660
And the reason they think that is that they watched the Gulf War.

513
00:42:15,940 --> 00:42:18,800
They saw the Patriots firing against the Scud missiles.

514
00:42:19,640 --> 00:42:25,300
And so they naturally think that if we could protect forces in a place like Saudi Arabia,

515
00:42:25,500 --> 00:42:27,400
we can obviously protect our population.

516
00:42:28,360 --> 00:42:32,700
Well, protecting against those kinds of missiles as opposed to the kinds of missiles,

517
00:42:32,701 --> 00:42:36,100
the ballistic missiles that I'm talking about that would come over great distances

518
00:42:36,101 --> 00:42:38,380
and travel very fast.

519
00:42:38,740 --> 00:42:41,140
We're talking about a whole different kind of a capability here.

520
00:42:41,141 --> 00:42:45,400
And clearly, we do not have that capability in this nation today.

521
00:42:46,680 --> 00:42:50,020
The U.S. government is currently studying the feasibility

522
00:42:50,021 --> 00:42:52,800
of creating an anti-ballistic missile defense system.

523
00:42:53,340 --> 00:42:57,200
If it is developed, most experts believe that it will probably be operated

524
00:42:57,201 --> 00:43:00,600
from Cheyenne Mountain, where its deployment would be supported

525
00:43:00,601 --> 00:43:03,940
by the assets of both NORAD and U.S. Space Command.

526
00:43:03,941 --> 00:43:08,940
Once again, NORAD's mission would be adapted to meet a new challenge.

527
00:43:08,941 --> 00:43:13,940
I believe NORAD still has a valid mission in the post-Cold War world.

528
00:43:13,941 --> 00:43:15,940
The nature of the threat changes.

529
00:43:15,941 --> 00:43:19,940
The nature of the technology to meet that threat also changes,

530
00:43:19,941 --> 00:43:22,940
and NORAD has demonstrated over the last 40 years

531
00:43:22,941 --> 00:43:26,940
a clear capability to adapt its mission to new threats.

532
00:43:26,941 --> 00:43:30,940
One of the main reasons why a sovereign nation feels the military

533
00:43:30,941 --> 00:43:32,940
is for protection of the homeland.

534
00:43:32,941 --> 00:43:40,200
and the things that we're doing in this mountain today are accomplishing those goals today

535
00:43:40,201 --> 00:43:44,040
and they're just as important today as they were 10 years ago

536
00:43:44,041 --> 00:43:46,700
and they'll be just as important 10 years from now.

537
00:43:47,200 --> 00:43:52,440
There's no question that NORAD will continue to play an important role in the future.

538
00:43:53,320 --> 00:43:56,120
But as one walks through this subterranean maze,

539
00:43:56,121 --> 00:44:02,060
it's hard not to think of the past and a frightening era when the unthinkable was thinkable

540
00:44:02,061 --> 00:44:06,120
and two mighty superpowers prepared for Armageddon.

541
00:44:07,820 --> 00:44:10,920
As one contemplates NORAD's significance,

542
00:44:11,200 --> 00:44:15,060
a truth emerges that is both simple and profound.

543
00:44:15,740 --> 00:44:17,280
We're all still here.

